DATA=[{'filename': 'como-decorar-balcon-pequeno.html', 'title': 'How to Decorate a Small Balcony and Make the Most of Every Inch', 'category': 'OUTDOOR LIVING', 'meta': 'Learn how to decorate a small balcony with compact furniture, vertical storage, plants, privacy, lighting, textiles, and practical weather-resistant solutions.', 'intro': 'A small balcony can become a comfortable extension of the home when every element has a clear purpose and the layout preserves circulation.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Measure the Balcony Before Decorating</li>\n<li>Define the Main Function</li>\n<li>Choose Compact and Flexible Furniture</li>\n<li>Use Vertical Space</li>\n<li>Add Plants Without Blocking Circulation</li>\n<li>Create Privacy Without Closing the Space</li>\n<li>Use Textiles and Color Carefully</li>\n<li>Improve Lighting for Evening Use</li>\n<li>Keep Storage Simple</li>\n<li>Protect the Balcony From Weather</li>\n<li>Common Mistakes to Avoid</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A small balcony can become a comfortable extension of the home when every element has a clear purpose. The key is not to fill the area with as many objects as possible, but to preserve circulation, choose furniture that matches the scale, use walls and railings intelligently, and add only the plants, textiles, lighting, and accessories that improve daily use.</p>\n<p>Before buying anything, measure the balcony carefully and observe sun, wind, privacy, and drainage. A piece that looks compact in a store may still block a door or leave too little room to move. Planning the layout first saves money and prevents the space from feeling crowded.</p>\n\n<h2>Measure the Balcony Before Decorating</h2>\n<p>Record the width, depth, door opening, railing height, drain positions, and any fixed elements such as air-conditioning equipment or utility boxes. These measurements determine what can realistically fit.</p>\n<p>Use painter's tape or cardboard to mark the footprint of proposed furniture. Then open the balcony door fully and walk through the space. This simple test shows whether a table, chair, or planter would interfere with circulation.</p>\n<p>Also consider the vertical dimensions. If you plan to use shelving or hanging plants, check head clearance and make sure nothing extends into a route where people could bump into it.</p>\n\n<h2>Define the Main Function</h2>\n<p>A very small balcony usually works better when it has one primary purpose. It might be a place for coffee, reading, plants, a compact dining area, or simply a calm outdoor corner.</p>\n<p>Trying to combine dining, storage, gardening, exercise equipment, and several seats in a limited area can make the balcony less useful. Choose the activity that matters most and let the other elements support it.</p>\n<p>If the space needs to change functions, use furniture that folds, stacks, or moves easily. Flexibility is more valuable than forcing several permanent zones into a small footprint.</p>\n\n<h2>Choose Compact and Flexible Furniture</h2>\n<p>Furniture should be proportionate to the balcony. Slim chairs, folding tables, narrow benches, and small stools can provide function without dominating the area.</p>\n<p>A wall-mounted or railing-mounted table may free floor space, but it must be installed securely and be appropriate for the structure. Follow manufacturer instructions and any building or rental restrictions.</p>\n<p>Stackable or folding chairs are useful when the balcony is used by one person most of the time but occasionally needs an extra seat. Store unused pieces so they do not permanently occupy valuable space.</p>\n<p>Choose outdoor-rated materials suitable for local sun, rain, humidity, and wind. A beautiful piece that deteriorates quickly can become more expensive than a simpler, durable option.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Vertical Space</h2>\n<p>Walls and railings can provide planting or storage opportunities without consuming the entire floor. Narrow shelves, secure hooks, or vertical planters can increase capacity.</p>\n<p>Do not overload walls or railings. Containers filled with wet soil are heavy, and all supports should be suitable for the expected load. Avoid placing heavy objects above seating or circulation areas unless the installation is designed for that purpose.</p>\n<p>Keep frequently used items at comfortable heights. A vertical system is only practical when plants can be watered and objects can be reached without awkward movement.</p>\n\n<h2>Add Plants Without Blocking Circulation</h2>\n<p>Plants can soften the balcony and create a stronger connection with nature. Choose species according to actual sunlight, wind exposure, climate, and the size of the containers you can accommodate.</p>\n<p>Group several pots together rather than scattering them along every edge. This creates a more intentional composition and makes watering easier.</p>\n<p>Tall plants can provide structure and some privacy, while trailing plants soften railings or shelves. Always consider mature size so a compact arrangement does not become overcrowded after a few months.</p>\n<p>Make sure drainage water does not create slippery surfaces or drip onto neighbors below. Use suitable saucers or trays where appropriate and respect building rules.</p>\n\n<h2>Create Privacy Without Closing the Space</h2>\n<p>Privacy can be improved with plants, outdoor curtains, screens, bamboo-like panels, or other suitable materials, but the solution should not block all light or airflow unnecessarily.</p>\n<p>Before attaching anything to a railing, check building rules and wind exposure. Lightweight materials can become dangerous if they are not secured properly.</p>\n<p>Partial screening is often enough. Covering only the most exposed side can create a more comfortable feeling while preserving openness and natural light.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Textiles and Color Carefully</h2>\n<p>Outdoor cushions, a small rug, or a throw can make the balcony feel more comfortable, but choose materials designed for exterior use or easy storage.</p>\n<p>In a very small area, a limited color palette helps the space feel more organized. Repeating two or three colors through cushions, pots, and accessories can create visual unity.</p>\n<p>A rug can define the seating zone, but it should dry quickly and not block drainage. If rain reaches the balcony regularly, a rug may require more maintenance than expected.</p>\n\n<h2>Improve Lighting for Evening Use</h2>\n<p>Soft lighting can extend the use of the balcony after sunset. String lights, portable outdoor lamps, or wall fixtures can create atmosphere when they are suitable for outdoor conditions.</p>\n<p>Avoid excessive brightness. Small balconies usually need only enough light for safe movement and comfortable seating.</p>\n<p>If electrical installation is required, use outdoor-rated equipment and follow applicable safety requirements. Solar lights can be useful when they receive enough sun to recharge effectively.</p>\n\n<h2>Keep Storage Simple</h2>\n<p>Storage should solve a real need without taking over the balcony. A bench with internal storage, a small waterproof box, or a narrow cabinet may be enough.</p>\n<p>Do not use the balcony as a permanent dumping area for items that do not belong elsewhere. Too much storage can quickly eliminate the outdoor feeling you are trying to create.</p>\n<p>Keep frequently used gardening or cleaning items accessible, and move seasonal or rarely used objects to another storage area when possible.</p>\n\n<h2>Protect the Balcony From Weather</h2>\n<p>Sun, wind, rain, and humidity affect furniture, plants, and textiles. Choose materials accordingly and store delicate items during severe weather.</p>\n<p>Strong wind deserves special attention. Lightweight chairs, empty pots, decorative objects, and unsecured textiles can move unexpectedly. Use stable furniture and secure loose items.</p>\n<p>Check drainage regularly. Leaves and soil can block outlets, causing water to accumulate. Keeping the floor clear around drains is an important part of balcony maintenance.</p>\n\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>One of the most common mistakes is buying oversized furniture. Always measure and test the footprint before purchasing.</p>\n<p>Another mistake is filling every empty surface. A small balcony needs visible space to feel comfortable. Leave enough room to move, open doors, and clean.</p>\n<p>Avoid choosing plants only for appearance. Sun, wind, and container size determine whether they will actually thrive.</p>\n<p>Finally, do not ignore building rules. Railings, external façades, hanging objects, and permanent installations may be subject to restrictions.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What furniture works best on a very small balcony?</h3>\n<p>Folding, stackable, narrow, or multifunctional furniture usually works well because it preserves floor area and can adapt to different uses.</p>\n<h3>How can I make a small balcony feel larger?</h3>\n<p>Keep circulation clear, use vertical space, limit the number of colors and materials, and avoid oversized furniture or too many accessories.</p>\n<h3>Can I create privacy with plants?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Tall or climbing plants can help filter views if they are suitable for the light, climate, container size, and maintenance conditions.</p>\n<h3>Should I use a rug outdoors?</h3>\n<p>Only if it suits the weather exposure and drainage of the balcony. Outdoor rugs can add comfort but may require regular drying and cleaning.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Make the Balcony Easy to Maintain</h2>\n<p>Design maintenance into the layout. Leave enough space to sweep, clean the railing, reach drains, and water plants without moving every piece of furniture. A compact balcony becomes frustrating when cleaning requires completely dismantling the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Review the balcony every few weeks. Remove dead plant material, wipe furniture, check fasteners, and make sure nothing has become unstable because of wind or weather. Small routine checks help preserve both appearance and safety.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Decorating a small balcony successfully depends on proportion and restraint. Measure first, define the main function, choose compact furniture, use vertical space, and add plants and textiles without blocking circulation.</p>\n<p>The best balcony is not the one with the most objects. It is the one that remains easy to move through, easy to maintain, and pleasant to use. With careful choices, even a very small outdoor area can become a comfortable and personal extension of the home.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-decorar-con-textiles-naturales-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Decorate With Natural Textiles at Home', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to decorate with natural textiles such as cotton, linen, wool, jute, and hemp through curtains, rugs, bedding, cushions, and coordinated textures.', 'intro': 'Natural textiles can make a home feel warmer, softer, and more comfortable when they are selected according to use, care, climate, and the existing design.', 'body': '\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Understand What Natural Textiles Are</li>\n<li>Choose the Right Fabric for Each Use</li>\n<li>Combine Textures Without Making the Room Heavy</li>\n<li>Use Natural Textiles in the Living Room</li>\n<li>Use Them in Bedrooms</li>\n<li>Bring Them Into the Kitchen and Dining Area</li>\n<li>Use Curtains to Soften Light</li>\n<li>Mix Natural Textiles With Wood, Stone, and Ceramics</li>\n<li>Care for Natural Fabrics Correctly</li>\n<li>Create a Cohesive Palette</li>\n<li>Common Mistakes to Avoid</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Natural textiles can make a home feel warmer, softer, and more comfortable. Materials such as cotton, linen, wool, jute, hemp, and other natural fibers introduce texture and visual depth while remaining practical when they are selected according to the room and the way the item will be used.</p>\n<p>The goal is not to fill the home with one material. Natural textiles work best when they are combined thoughtfully with existing furniture, colors, wood, stone, metal, and ceramics. A few well-chosen fabrics can transform the atmosphere without requiring major renovation.</p>\n\n<h2>Understand What Natural Textiles Are</h2>\n<p>Natural textiles are produced from plant- or animal-based fibers. Cotton and linen are common examples, while wool, jute, and hemp provide different textures and performance characteristics.</p>\n<p>However, a product marketed with a natural look is not always made entirely from natural fibers. Check the material label if composition matters to you. Blended fabrics may combine natural and synthetic fibers to improve durability, stretch, or ease of care.</p>\n<p>There is no single best fiber. The appropriate choice depends on softness, resistance, moisture, cleaning requirements, climate, and how frequently the item will be used.</p>\n\n<h2>Choose the Right Fabric for Each Use</h2>\n<p>Lightweight cotton or linen can work well for curtains, cushion covers, or bedding. Wool can add warmth through rugs or throws. Jute and similar fibers may suit decorative rugs or baskets, although they may feel rougher and need different care.</p>\n<p>For frequently used upholstery, durability matters as much as appearance. A delicate fabric may be beautiful but impractical in a home with children, pets, or heavy everyday use.</p>\n<p>Always check care instructions. Some natural fibers can shrink, wrinkle, or react to moisture and heat. Choosing a fabric that fits your real cleaning routine prevents frustration later.</p>\n\n<h2>Combine Textures Without Making the Room Heavy</h2>\n<p>Texture is one of the main advantages of natural textiles. A linen cushion, woven wool throw, and jute rug can create depth even when the colors are neutral.</p>\n<p>Do not use every texture at once. Select two or three main materials and repeat them in different parts of the room. This creates continuity and keeps the composition calm.</p>\n<p>Balance rough and smooth surfaces. A heavily textured rug can be paired with simpler curtains, while soft bedding can sit beside a more structured woven basket.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Natural Textiles in the Living Room</h2>\n<p>The living room offers many opportunities to use natural fabrics through cushions, throws, curtains, rugs, and upholstered pieces.</p>\n<p>If the sofa is visually heavy, lighter linen-like cushions can soften it. If the room feels too plain, a textured wool or flat-weave rug can add interest.</p>\n<p>Choose colors that relate to the existing furniture. Natural textiles do not need to be beige or white. Deep green, rust, blue, terracotta, and other colors can work beautifully when they repeat tones already present in the room.</p>\n<p>Keep frequently used textiles easy to clean. Cushion covers with removable closures can be more practical than decorative covers that cannot be washed easily.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Them in Bedrooms</h2>\n<p>Bedrooms benefit from soft, breathable materials. Cotton and linen bedding can create a relaxed look, while wool blankets or textured throws add warmth during cooler seasons.</p>\n<p>Layer fabrics according to comfort rather than appearance alone. Too many heavy textiles can make a bedroom feel visually crowded and may be impractical in warm climates.</p>\n<p>Curtains, bedding, and rugs do not need to match exactly. A coordinated palette with different textures usually creates a more natural, lived-in result.</p>\n\n<h2>Bring Them Into the Kitchen and Dining Area</h2>\n<p>Natural textiles can appear through tablecloths, runners, napkins, chair cushions, and small rugs. In these areas, washable materials are especially important because spills are common.</p>\n<p>Linen and cotton table textiles can add texture while remaining relatively easy to store and rotate. Choose colors and patterns that work with dishes, wood tones, and the overall style of the room.</p>\n<p>Avoid placing delicate rugs in areas where grease, water, or heavy food traffic will make cleaning difficult. Practicality should guide placement.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Curtains to Soften Light</h2>\n<p>Curtains are one of the most visible textile elements in a room. Linen or cotton fabrics can filter daylight and soften the edges of a window.</p>\n<p>Choose the weight according to privacy and light control. A light fabric may be enough in a living room, while a bedroom may require a layered solution with a more opaque curtain or blind.</p>\n<p>Measure carefully before buying. Curtain length and fullness affect the finished look. A fabric that is too narrow can appear weak even if the material itself is attractive.</p>\n\n<h2>Mix Natural Textiles With Wood, Stone, and Ceramics</h2>\n<p>Natural fabrics combine especially well with other materials that have visible texture. Wood adds warmth, stone contributes weight and permanence, and ceramics introduce smooth or handcrafted surfaces.</p>\n<p>Use repetition to connect these materials. A jute rug can relate to a woven basket, while linen curtains can echo the soft texture of a ceramic lamp or wooden furniture.</p>\n<p>The objective is not to create a themed room but to build a consistent material palette. A few repeated textures usually feel more sophisticated than many unrelated natural-looking objects.</p>\n\n<h2>Care for Natural Fabrics Correctly</h2>\n<p>Always follow the care label. Cotton, linen, wool, and jute can respond differently to water, temperature, detergent, and drying.</p>\n<p>Some fabrics may shrink if washed too hot, while wool can felt if treated incorrectly. Jute and similar fibers may be damaged by excessive moisture.</p>\n<p>Vacuum rugs regularly using a method suitable for the fiber. Rotate rugs periodically when appropriate to help distribute wear and sunlight exposure more evenly.</p>\n<p>Address spills promptly, but do not scrub aggressively without knowing the correct method. Blotting and using a compatible cleaning approach is often safer.</p>\n\n<h2>Create a Cohesive Palette</h2>\n<p>Natural textiles work well in both neutral and colorful interiors. The key is repetition. Choose a limited number of main colors and repeat them across cushions, curtains, rugs, and bedding.</p>\n<p>Use texture to create variation within that palette. Two fabrics can be the same color but look different because one is smooth and another is heavily woven.</p>\n<p>If the room already contains strong furniture or wall colors, let the textiles support them instead of competing. A calm base can make more distinctive materials stand out.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Natural Textiles Seasonally</h2>\n<p>Textiles are easy to rotate according to weather and mood. Lighter cotton or linen fabrics can feel comfortable during warm months, while heavier wool or thick woven throws can add warmth in cooler seasons.</p>\n<p>Store off-season items clean and completely dry. Breathable storage and protection from moisture can help preserve them.</p>\n<p>Seasonal rotation also allows you to refresh the room without buying new furniture. Changing a throw, cushion cover, or table runner can be enough to create a noticeable difference.</p>\n\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>One mistake is assuming that every natural fiber is automatically durable or easy to clean. Performance varies widely, so check the material and care instructions.</p>\n<p>Another mistake is using too many rough textures together. Balance is important, especially in small rooms where every material is visually prominent.</p>\n<p>Avoid purchasing a large textile item before measuring. Rugs and curtains strongly affect proportion, so the wrong size can make a room feel awkward even if the material is beautiful.</p>\n<p>Finally, do not choose textiles only for appearance. Comfort, cleaning, durability, and climate all influence whether they will work well in everyday life.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Which natural textile is easiest to use at home?</h3>\n<p>Cotton is widely used because it is versatile, but the best choice depends on the item, required durability, and care instructions.</p>\n<h3>Do natural textiles only work with rustic decoration?</h3>\n<p>No. Linen, cotton, wool, jute, and other natural fibers can work in modern, minimalist, traditional, or eclectic interiors depending on color, weave, and styling.</p>\n<h3>Can I mix different natural fibers?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Mixing several fibers can create depth, especially when the colors and scale are coordinated.</p>\n<h3>Are natural textiles difficult to maintain?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily. Maintenance varies by fiber. Following the care label and choosing materials appropriate for the room makes upkeep much easier.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Shop More Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>Before buying new textiles, compare them with what you already own. A room may need only one new element rather than a complete set. Reusing and relocating cushions, throws, or curtains can reduce spending and prevent visual overload.</p>\n<p>Check dimensions, fiber composition, washing instructions, and color under the actual room lighting before making a large purchase. Practical information is just as important as appearance.</p>\n\n<h2>Shop More Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>Before buying new textiles, compare them with what you already own. A room may need only one new element rather than a complete set. Reusing and relocating cushions, throws, or curtains can reduce spending and prevent visual overload.</p>\n<p>Check dimensions, fiber composition, washing instructions, and color under the actual room lighting before making a large purchase. Practical information is just as important as appearance.</p>\n\n<h2>Shop More Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>Before buying new textiles, compare them with what you already own. A room may need only one new element rather than a complete set. Reusing and relocating cushions, throws, or curtains can reduce spending and prevent visual overload.</p>\n<p>Check dimensions, fiber composition, washing instructions, and color under the actual room lighting before making a large purchase. Practical information is just as important as appearance.</p>\n\n<h2>Shop More Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>Before buying new textiles, compare them with what you already own. A room may need only one new element rather than a complete set. Reusing and relocating cushions, throws, or curtains can reduce spending and prevent visual overload.</p>\n<p>Check dimensions, fiber composition, washing instructions, and color under the actual room lighting before making a large purchase. Practical information is just as important as appearance.</p>\n\n<h2>Shop More Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>Before buying new textiles, compare them with what you already own. A room may need only one new element rather than a complete set. Reusing and relocating cushions, throws, or curtains can reduce spending and prevent visual overload.</p>\n<p>Check dimensions, fiber composition, washing instructions, and color under the actual room lighting before making a large purchase. Practical information is just as important as appearance.</p>\n\n<h2>Shop More Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>Before buying new textiles, compare them with what you already own. A room may need only one new element rather than a complete set. Reusing and relocating cushions, throws, or curtains can reduce spending and prevent visual overload.</p>\n<p>Check dimensions, fiber composition, washing instructions, and color under the actual room lighting before making a large purchase. Practical information is just as important as appearance.</p>\n\n<h2>Shop More Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>Before buying new textiles, compare them with what you already own. A room may need only one new element rather than a complete set. Reusing and relocating cushions, throws, or curtains can reduce spending and prevent visual overload.</p>\n<p>Check dimensions, fiber composition, washing instructions, and color under the actual room lighting before making a large purchase. Practical information is just as important as appearance.</p>\n\n<h2>Shop More Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>Before buying new textiles, compare them with what you already own. A room may need only one new element rather than a complete set. Reusing and relocating cushions, throws, or curtains can reduce spending and prevent visual overload.</p>\n<p>Check dimensions, fiber composition, washing instructions, and color under the actual room lighting before making a large purchase. Practical information is just as important as appearance.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Decorating with natural textiles is an effective way to add warmth, texture, and comfort to a home. Cotton, linen, wool, jute, hemp, and other fibers can appear through curtains, rugs, bedding, cushions, and table textiles.</p>\n<p>The best results come from choosing each material according to use, care, and climate, then repeating a limited palette of colors and textures. Natural textiles do not need to dominate the room. A few well-chosen pieces can make the entire home feel softer, more layered, and more personal.</p>\n'}]

def wc(h):
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b", re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", h)))

